WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.091 --> 00:00:03.508
(lively orchestra music)

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.100
- Embalming students have
to understand the importance

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.803
that every face is different.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.000
Morgan Freeman, square face.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.070
Abraham Lincoln, oblong face.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.713
Kanye West, oval face.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.180
My name is James Edward Smith Jr.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.260
I'm an instructor and clinical director at

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.850
Dallas Institute of Funeral Service,

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.583
and I currently instruct restorative art.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.560
Restorative art class provides a skill set

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.450
to be able to take a deceased person

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.050
who has experienced trauma
to the head and/or face

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.340
and restore those features in that face,

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.520
so that the family may have an option

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.373
for an open casket viewing or visitation.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.110
In the fifth quarter, the
students are tasked with

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.370
reproducing a sculpture of
a person that they choose

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.493
and the anatomy has to be accurate.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.550
Students generally pick
celebrities, you know,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.220
they're popular faces and
students tend to want to

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.340
try an recreate those.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.520
- For my restorative art project I did

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.370
Jennifer Aniston.
- Jack White.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.200
- Viola Davis.
- Frida Kahlo.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.770
- I decided to do David Lynch.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.910
- Taking on the assignment
requires each of the students

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:32.420
to understand the proportions
for the features of the face

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.470
here in school because it translates to

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.483
what may happen in the
actual preparation room.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.800
It is an acquired skill.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.160
The more you practice,
the more proficient,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.710
the better you're gonna become.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.040
The expectation of the family is

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.610
that this is something we
can sometimes do right away.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.250
But we have to take into
account the amount of trauma

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.470
that the face has sustained.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:01.280
We need to have as much care
when handling the deceased

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.033
as if they were in the hospital
under the care of a doctor.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.870
I am very passionate about restorative art

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.840
because I believe it's
important for people

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.070
to have the best memory picture possible

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.570
when it's time to say goodbye.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.320
We're tasked, as
embalmers, to help families

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.330
have the option, no
matter the cause of death,

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:32.833
to say goodbye to Mom or
Dad or brother or sister.

00:02:38.628 --> 00:02:41.128
(tone chimes)

